from tnsoc import *
S(9,0,"Asha found a stone tool in a museum. \"Who made this?\" she asked.",
 ["History is the study of the human past. The Prehistoric period, before writing, is divided into the Stone Age (Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Neolithic) and the Metal Age. Early humans were hunter-gatherers who used stone tools and learned to make fire.",
  "In the Neolithic age people began farming and settled in villages, and they made pottery and domesticated animals. Later the Chalcolithic, Bronze and Iron ages brought metal tools. Evidence comes from fossils, tools, cave paintings and burial sites, and sites such as Adichanallur in Tamil Nadu show early life in the south.",
  "Human evolution passed from Australopithecus to Homo habilis, Homo erectus and Homo sapiens. Sources of history are archaeological and literary, and archaeologists study the remains of the past."],
 ["prehistory","Stone Age","Neolithic","hunter-gatherers","fossils","archaeology"],
 ("Why was farming a turning point in history?",["People could grow food in one place.","They settled in villages.","This led to growth of towns and trade."]),
 ("Early humans lived in cities.",["They were hunter-gatherers.","Cities came much later."]),
 ["Prehistory is before writing.","Neolithic means farming and settled life.","Evidence: fossils, tools, paintings.","Homo sapiens is modern humans."],
 ["m|The period before writing is|prehistory|medieval|modern|No written records.","t|Neolithic people practised farming.|T|Yes.","m|Early humans got food by|hunting and gathering|factory work|trade only|Before farming.","t|Fire was unknown in the Stone Age.|F|Humans learned to make fire.","m|Modern humans are|Homo sapiens|Homo erectus|Australopithecus|Present species.","s|Which age came first, Stone or Iron?|stone|Stone is earlier."])
S(9,1,"Meena saw a picture of Mohenjo-daro's great bath. \"Cities this old?\" she wondered.",
 ["Ancient civilisations grew along great rivers: Mesopotamia on the Tigris and Euphrates, Egypt on the Nile, the Indus Valley and China on the Yellow River. Rivers gave fertile land and water for farming and travel.",
  "The Indus Valley civilisation had well-planned cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, with grid streets, drainage and a great bath. The Egyptians built pyramids and wrote in hieroglyphics, while Mesopotamians used cuneiform writing and the Chinese developed silk and the calendar.",
  "These civilisations had trade, writing, art and organised government. They left behind monuments and artefacts that tell us how people lived."],
 ["civilisation","Mesopotamia","Indus Valley","hieroglyphics","cuneiform","drainage"],
 ("Why did early civilisations begin near rivers?",["Rivers give water and fertile soil.","They help transport and trade.","So large settlements could grow."]),
 ("Harappan cities had no planning.",["They had grid streets and drains.","They were well planned."]),
 ["Early civilisations grew by rivers.","Indus cities were planned.","Egyptians wrote hieroglyphics.","Mesopotamia used cuneiform."],
 ["m|Mohenjo-daro belongs to the|Indus Valley civilisation|Egyptian civilisation|Chinese civilisation|Indus site.","t|Egyptians built pyramids.|T|Yes.","m|Cuneiform was used in|Mesopotamia|Egypt|China|Wedge-shaped writing.","t|Civilisations grew far from rivers.|F|They grew near rivers.","m|Hieroglyphics is the writing of|Egypt|India|Rome|Picture writing.","s|Which river is linked to Egypt?|nile|The Nile."])
S(9,2,"Raju read a Tamil poem about the sea and ships. \"Tamils traded long ago?\" he asked.",
 ["Early Tamil society is known from Sangam literature, such as Ettuthogai and Pathupattu. The Tamil region was divided into five landscapes called Thinai: Kurinji (hills), Mullai (forest), Marutham (farmland), Neithal (coast) and Palai (dry land).",
  "The Cheras, Cholas and Pandyas ruled early Tamilakam. Trade with Rome, Greece and South-East Asia flourished through ports such as Muziris, Korkai and Kaveripattinam. Archaeological sites like Keeladi, Arikamedu and Adichanallur give evidence of early urban life.",
  "Sangam society valued poetry, music and dance. Women poets such as Avvaiyar are remembered, and a code of conduct for love and war, called Akam and Puram, shaped the literature."],
 ["Sangam literature","Thinai","Chera, Chola, Pandya","Keeladi","Muziris","Akam and Puram"],
 ("How do we know about early Tamil society?",["Sangam literature describes life.","Archaeology gives physical evidence like Keeladi.","Foreign accounts speak of trade."]),
 ("Tamils never traded overseas.",["They traded with Rome and South-East Asia.","Ports like Muziris show this."]),
 ["Sangam literature is a main source.","Five Thinai landscapes.","Three kingdoms: Chera, Chola, Pandya.","Keeladi shows urban life."],
 ["m|The three early Tamil kingdoms were|Chera, Chola, Pandya|Maurya, Gupta, Chola|Pallava, Pandya, Mughal|Sangam rulers.","t|Kurinji stands for hills.|T|Yes.","m|Which site shows early Tamil urban life?|Keeladi|Taxila|Kalinga|Near Madurai.","t|Sangam poems are about hunting only.|F|They deal with love, war, ethics.","m|Muziris was a|port|mountain|desert|Trade centre.","s|Name a Sangam woman poet.|avvaiyar|Famous poet."])
S(9,3,"Asha heard that Buddha and Mahavira lived about the same time. \"Why so many thinkers then?\" she asked.",
 ["Around the sixth century BCE new ideas arose in India. Jainism, taught by Mahavira, stresses non-violence, truth and non-possession. Buddhism, taught by Gautama Buddha, speaks of the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path to end suffering.",
  "The same time saw thinkers elsewhere: Confucius in China, Socrates and Plato in Greece and Zoroaster in Persia. Kingdoms called Mahajanapadas grew in north India, and Magadha rose to power.",
  "Ashoka the Maurya spread Buddhism and the message of dhamma after the Kalinga war. Tamil culture had its own thought, and the Thirukkural, with its teachings on virtue, wealth and love, came to be treasured."],
 ["Jainism","Buddhism","Four Noble Truths","Mahajanapadas","Ashoka","Thirukkural"],
 ("Why did Ashoka turn to dhamma?",["The Kalinga war caused great suffering.","He felt remorse.","He spread peace and non-violence."]),
 ("Buddha taught about the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path in Greece.",["Buddha taught in India.","The Four Noble Truths are Buddhist."]),
 ["New religions arose in the 6th century BCE.","Mahavira: Jainism.","Buddha: Four Noble Truths.","Ashoka spread dhamma."],
 ["m|Jainism was taught by|Mahavira|Buddha|Ashoka|Jain tirthankara.","t|Ashoka spread dhamma.|T|Yes.","m|The Eightfold Path is a Buddhist|path to end suffering|tax|army|Buddha's teaching.","t|Confucius lived in India.|F|He lived in China.","m|The Mahajanapada that rose to power was|Magadha|Vanga|Sindh|Later the Mauryas.","s|Which war changed Ashoka?|kalinga|Kalinga war."])
S(9,4,"Meena saw the ruins of Athens in a book. \"Democracy began there,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The classical world refers mainly to ancient Greece and Rome. Greece was made of city-states such as Athens and Sparta. Athens developed an early form of democracy in which citizens took part in the assembly.",
  "Greek thinkers like Socrates, Plato and Aristotle founded philosophy, and Hippocrates is called the father of medicine. Alexander the Great of Macedon built a vast empire that spread Greek culture.",
  "Rome began as a republic and became an empire under Augustus. The Romans built roads, aqueducts and the Colosseum and gave us Roman law and Latin. The empire later weakened and fell in the 5th century CE."],
 ["city-state","democracy","philosophy","Alexander","republic","Roman law"],
 ("Why is Athens called the home of democracy?",["Citizens met in an assembly.","They voted on laws.","This was an early form of rule by the people."]),
 ("The Roman Empire never fell.",["It fell in the 5th century CE.","It had weakened earlier."]),
 ["Greece: city-states.","Athens: early democracy.","Rome: republic then empire.","Romans built roads and gave law."],
 ["m|Athens is known for|democracy|monarchy only|pyramids|Citizen assembly.","t|Aristotle was a Greek thinker.|T|Yes.","m|The Colosseum is in|Rome|Athens|Cairo|Roman arena.","t|Alexander was a Roman emperor.|F|He was Macedonian.","m|Rome first was a|republic|dictatorship only|colony|Later an empire.","s|Who is called the father of medicine?|hippocrates|Greek physician."])
S(9,5,"Raju read about knights in armour. \"Were there really knights?\" he asked.",
 ["The Middle Ages in Europe followed the fall of Rome. A system called feudalism arose, with kings giving land to lords, who gave land to knights, who protected peasants called serfs. The Church had great power.",
  "The Crusades were holy wars between Christians and Muslims over Jerusalem. Trade and towns grew again, and guilds of merchants and craftsmen formed. In the east, Islam spread and the Arab world became a centre of learning in science and mathematics.",
  "Later the Black Death killed many people and weakened feudalism. New universities, such as Bologna and Oxford, appeared."],
 ["Middle Ages","feudalism","serf","Crusades","guild","university"],
 ("How did feudalism work?",["Kings gave land to lords.","Lords gave land to knights for protection.","Serfs worked the land."]),
 ("Serfs were powerful lords.",["Serfs worked the land for lords.","Lords held power."]),
 ["Feudalism was a system of land and loyalty.","Church was powerful.","Crusades: wars for Jerusalem.","Towns and guilds grew."],
 ["m|Feudalism was a system based on|land and loyalty|factories|trade only|Lords and vassals.","t|Serfs worked on the lord's land.|T|Yes.","m|The Crusades were fought over|Jerusalem|Rome|Paris|Holy land.","t|Guilds were groups of farmers only.|F|Of merchants and craftsmen.","m|The Black Death was a|plague|war|law|Killed many people.","s|Who protected peasants in feudalism?|knights|Under lords."])
